<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Kel Adagh</h1><p class="st-meta">1700 CE – 1910 CE (approx.) · capital Kidal</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Tuareg confederation of the Adrar des Ifoghas mountains around Kidal, led by the Ifoghas lineage and central to Tuareg politics in Mali.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Kel Adagh, meaning the people of the mountain, formed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adrar-des-ifoghas">Adrar des Ifoghas</a> massif of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>. By the 18th century the Ifoghas, a lineage of religious prestige, had become the leading group, providing the confederation's amenokal.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The confederation united noble, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> and dependent groups of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> and goat herders who moved between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> and pastures in a harsh environment. It traded livestock and salt with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> valley and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>, and maintained shifting relations with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kel-ahaggar">Kel Ahaggar</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/iwellemmeden-kel-ataram">Iwellemmeden Kel Ataram</a>. Islamic scholarship gave the Ifoghas authority.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>French forces occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adrar-des-ifoghas">Adrar des Ifoghas</a> around 1908 to 1910 and ruled through the amenokal. The confederation became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-sudan">French Sudan</a>, and after independence <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kidal">Kidal</a> was the centre of repeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> rebellions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 1700 CE – 1910 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44